---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// Written because the rest of the site tells people to verify the privacy
// claims rather than trust them (see blogs/my-plan.astro). Counting traffic
// without saying so plainly would undercut that. No `active` value: the footer
// links here, the nav doesn't.
---
<Base
  title="Privacy — LightPhotos"
  desc="What LightPhotos counts on this website, what it deliberately does not, and why the desktop app makes no network requests at all."
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <h1>Privacy</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          This site counts visits and browser app usage. Here is exactly
          where that line sits, in plain terms.
        </p>
      </header>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        There are no cookies on this site, no accounts, no advertising networks
        and no third-party analytics service. Events go to a first-party endpoint hosted by Cloudflare and are stored
        in our Cloudflare D1 database. We do not send events to advertising networks.
      </p>

      <h2>What the website records</h2>
      <p>
        When you load a page here, it records the page&rsquo;s path, the site
        you followed a link from &mdash; the domain only, never the full
        address &mdash; your country, and a daily pseudonymous visitor number described
        below. It also records a few named events: that a download button was
        clicked and which platform it was for, and whether the web app finished
        loading.
      </p>
      <p>
        The browser app also records folder opens, rating changes, committed
        edits (including Auto Tone), filter changes, successful exports, and
        categorized decode or export failures. Startup and first-photo timing
        help identify reliability problems. First-photo time includes time
        spent choosing a folder; it is not a decode benchmark. Session length
        and photo counts use coarse bands. Error messages are never sent.
      </p>

      <h2>How visitors are counted without cookies</h2>
      <p>
        Counting people normally means giving each browser a lasting identifier
        and storing it. This site doesn&rsquo;t. Instead the server takes your
        IP address, your browser&rsquo;s user-agent string, today&rsquo;s date
        and a random number generated fresh each morning, and runs the lot
        through a one-way hash. What gets stored is the result &mdash; a short
        pseudonymous string rather than the original IP address.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because the date and that day&rsquo;s random number are both part of
        the hash, the number representing you changes at midnight and
        tomorrow&rsquo;s cannot be connected to today&rsquo;s. So it can count
        how many distinct people visited on a given day, and it cannot follow
        anyone from one day to the next. Your IP address and user-agent are
        used to compute the hash and are then discarded. Neither is written
        down.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each day&rsquo;s random number is itself deleted after two days. That
        matters: while it exists, someone holding the database could in
        principle confirm a guess about one specific IP address by recomputing
        the hash. Once it is gone, that stops being possible at all, for
        anyone, permanently &mdash; including for me. Events themselves are
        deleted after a year.
      </p>

      <h2>What is never recorded</h2>
      <ul class="lp-check">
        <li>No cookies, and nothing stored in your browser</li>
        <li>No IP addresses or user-agent strings kept after hashing</li>
        <li>No tracking across days, across sites, or across devices</li>
        <li>No advertising trackers; event storage is hosted by Cloudflare, and data is not sold</li>
        <li>Nothing about your photos &mdash; no filenames, folders, paths or image data, ever</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The desktop app makes no network requests</h2>
      <p>
        This page is about the website. The downloadable app is a different
        matter, and the answer there is simpler: it does not talk to the
        network at all. The code that reports anything is compiled only into
        the browser build. For the desktop build it does not exist &mdash; not
        disabled by a setting you have to trust, but absent from the binary.
      </p>
      <p>
        The web app, running at <a href="/app.html">/app.html</a>, does report
        which features get used &mdash; that a folder was opened, that a photo
        was rated, that an export ran. Those are counts and categories only.
        The number of photos in a folder is recorded as a rough band rather
        than an exact figure, and never the folder, never the filenames, never
        the images. Your photos are read by the browser on your own machine and
        are not uploaded, in either version.
      </p>

      <h2>Opting out</h2>
      <p>
        If your browser sends Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, this site
        records nothing at all &mdash; not even a page view. No banner, no
        preference to set here. Any content blocker that stops the request will
        also work, and nothing on the site breaks when it does.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        If something here is unclear or you think it is wrong, open an issue on
        <a href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/issues">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
